# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/society.grim
# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after "="; keep {tokens}.
# "# en:" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;
# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.


@thread the_season
# en: The Season
name [eb00c9] = 社交季
# en: The patrons had noticed you. Their round of suppers and their ball had a place laid for you, if you wanted it.
premise [16d723] = 赞助人们注意到了你。他们的一轮晚宴和舞会上，为你留了一个位置，只要你想要。
# en: An invitation came with a crest on it: the patrons' Season had a place laid for you.
journal.1 [4d819c] = 一张带纹章的请柬寄来了：赞助人们的社交季为你留了一个位置。
# en: At the first supper you were the talk of the table.
journal.2 [c2424b] = 第一场晚宴上，你成了全桌的话题。
# en: At the first supper you were plainly yourself, and some of the table liked it.
journal.3 [02390b] = 第一场晚宴上，你朴朴实实地做你自己，桌上有些人喜欢这样。
# en: Lady Vaneshade offered to sponsor you, and you let her.
journal.4 [4e190f] = 凡妮希德夫人提出要做你的引荐人，你答应了。
# en: Lady Vaneshade offered to sponsor you, and you thanked her and said no.
journal.5 [1d0bd4] = 凡妮希德夫人提出要做你的引荐人，你谢过她，婉拒了。
# en: At the patrons' ball, you outshone the Season's other darling.
journal.6 [ba7e67] = 赞助人舞会上，你的风头盖过了社交季的另一位宠儿。
# en: At the patrons' ball, you shared the floor with the Season's other darling.
journal.7 [a12dec] = 赞助人舞会上，你和社交季的另一位宠儿共享了舞池。
# en: A patron asked a favour in return, and you did it.
journal.8 [4a9a2f] = 一位赞助人要你回报一个人情，你照办了。
# en: A patron asked a favour in return, and you refused.
journal.9 [c95cf3] = 一位赞助人要你回报一个人情，你拒绝了。
# en: The Season ended with a standing invitation. You belong among them now, a little.
journal.10 [8b9820] = 社交季结束时你收到了一份长期邀请。如今你多少算是他们中的一员了。
# en: The Season ended, and you went back up the hill and didn't look back.
journal.11 [1fa682] = 社交季结束了，你回到山上，再也没有回头。

@event season_invitation
# en: A Card With a Crest
title [505a5a] = 一张带纹章的请柬
# en: The card is heavy, cream and gilt-edged, and it has your name on it in a hand that has written a great many names. The patrons of the academy, it says, would be delighted to see you at the Season: a round of suppers in the town houses below the hill, and a ball at the end of it. People who go to the Season come back different. People say so as if it were a warning.
text [d50b17] = 这张请柬厚重，奶油色，镶着金边，上面写着你的名字，那笔迹写过许许多多的名字。请柬上说，学院的赞助人们很乐意在社交季见到你：在山下的宅邸里办一轮晚宴，最后以一场舞会收尾。去过社交季的人回来都变了个样。人们这么说的时候，像是在提醒什么。
# en: A heavy cream card with a gilt crest lying on a dormitory bed beside a pile of textbooks, a student holding it up to the light.
scene [8147ac] = 宿舍床上，一张带镀金纹章的厚重奶油色请柬放在一堆课本旁边，一个学生把它举到灯下看。
# en: Accept
opt.left.label [b26149] = 接受
# en: You write back in your best hand and spend more than you meant on a decent coat. Word gets round the castle that you're going. Your friends are impressed and a little wary. "Don't come back talking about wine," one of them says.
opt.left.say.1 [429a69] = 你用最好的字迹写了回信，还在一件像样的外套上花了比预想多的钱。你要去的消息传遍了城堡。你的朋友们既佩服，又有点提防。“回来可别满嘴都是葡萄酒，”其中一个说。
# en: Send your regrets
opt.right.label [c24080] = 婉言谢绝
# en: You send a polite note saying you're afraid your studies won't allow it. It's half true. The card goes in a drawer, and now and then you take it out and look at the crest.
opt.right.say.1 [79acc5] = 你写了张客气的便条，说恐怕学业不允许。这有一半是真的。请柬进了抽屉，你时不时拿出来看看那枚纹章。

@event season_first_supper
# en: The First Supper
title [877e01] = 第一场晚宴
# en: The first supper is twelve courses in a town house with a ceiling painted with clouds. You're seated between a retired magistrate and a woman who owns half the orchards in the valley. Both of them have read about you. Both of them want to see whether you're as interesting as the Ledger said. The magistrate has an opinion on every course, and is plainly waiting for somebody to disagree with him.
text [c4f8ef] = 第一场晚宴有十二道菜，在一座天花板上画着云的宅邸里。你坐在一位退休的地方法官和一位拥有山谷里一半果园的女士中间。他们俩都读过关于你的报道。他们俩都想看看你是不是像《纪事报》说的那么有意思。那位地方法官对每道菜都有意见，显然正等着有人跟他唱反调。
# en: A long candlelit table in a townhouse dining room, silver and crystal everywhere, a student in a new coat seated between two elderly patrons in jewels.
scene [ee05b8] = 宅邸餐厅里一张点着蜡烛的长桌，到处是银器和水晶，一个穿着新外套的学生坐在两位珠光宝气的年长赞助人中间。
# en: Argue with the magistrate
opt.left.label [1b006f] = 跟地方法官争一争
# en: You tell the magistrate he's wrong about the soup, and then about a famous old trial, and he's delighted: nobody has disagreed with him since he retired. By the ninth course the whole table is listening to the two of you. Someone at the far end asks your name twice, to remember it.
opt.left.say.1 [7dfb29] = 你告诉地方法官他对那道汤的看法不对，接着又说他对一场著名旧审判的看法也不对，他高兴极了：自打他退休，就再没人跟他唱过反调。到第九道菜，整张桌子都在听你们俩争。远处有人问了你两遍名字，好记住它。
# en: You take the magistrate on over an old case, and turn out to have the facts backwards, in front of the man who heard it. The orchard woman changes the subject, kindly, which is worse. You spend the last three courses very interested in your soup.
opt.left.say.2 [aabc3a] = 你拿一桩旧案跟地方法官较劲，结果发现自己把案情记反了，而且是当着审过那桩案子的人的面。那位果园女士和气地岔开了话题，这更糟。最后三道菜，你对你的汤表现出了浓厚的兴趣。
# en: Ask about her orchards
opt.right.label [9d9b2c] = 问问她的果园
# en: You leave the magistrate to the soup. You ask the orchard woman about her orchards, and she talks for an hour, delighted, about apple blight. The table thinks you're a little dull. She thinks you're the best guest she's had in years.
opt.right.say.1 [d42897] = 你让地方法官去对付他的汤。你向那位果园女士问起她的果园，她高高兴兴地讲了一个小时的苹果枯萎病。桌上的人觉得你有点无趣。她觉得你是她这些年来最好的客人。

@event season_lady_vaneshade
# en: Lady Vaneshade's Offer
title [4e8a64] = 凡妮希德夫人的提议
# en: At the second supper, Lady Vaneshade{met(lyra): , Lyra's mother,|} takes you aside into a drawing room. "You'll want a sponsor," she says. "Someone to tell you which fork, and which people. I could be that. In return, you'll be mine: seen with my family, and not with anyone I'd rather you weren't." She doesn't say who that is. She doesn't have to.
text [b42cd2] = 第二场晚宴上，凡妮希德夫人{met(lyra): ，莱拉的母亲，|}把你带到一间客厅里。“你会需要一位引荐人，”她说，“告诉你该用哪把叉子，该见哪些人。我可以做这个人。作为回报，你得是我的人：和我的家族一起露面，不和任何我不愿看到的人在一起。”她没说那是谁。她也用不着说。
# en: A tall woman in crimson and black in a drawing room by a fire, holding a glass she isn't drinking from, a student standing very straight before her.
scene [744151] = 炉火边的客厅里，一位穿深红和黑色衣裳的高个女人端着一杯没喝的酒，一个学生笔直地站在她面前。
# en: Accept her sponsorship
opt.left.label [17b382] = 接受她的引荐
# en: "Good," she says, and from then on doors open before you reach them. She tells you which forks. She also tells you, once, very quietly, who not to be seen with, and you find that you've done as she says before you've thought about it.
opt.left.say.1 [b413fa] = “很好，”她说，从那以后，门总在你走到之前就打开了。她告诉你该用哪把叉子。有一次她也非常小声地告诉你不该和谁一起露面，而你发现，你还没想清楚，就已经照她说的做了。
# en: Thank her, and say no
opt.right.label [ecf60d] = 谢过她，然后拒绝
# en: "I'd rather find my own forks," you say. Lady Vaneshade looks at you for a long moment, then smiles, not warmly. "Most people do," she says. "Most people find the wrong ones." The doors stay shut a little longer after that.
opt.right.say.1 [86902a] = “我宁可自己找叉子，”你说。凡妮希德夫人看了你好一会儿，然后笑了，笑得并不温暖。“大多数人都这么想，”她说，“大多数人找错了。”从那以后，那些门开得慢了些。

@event season_rival
# en: The Season's Other Darling
title [8829ce] = 社交季的另一位宠儿
# en: The Season always has a darling, and this one had one before you came: a young duelling champion from the city, handsome, charming and very used to being the most interesting person in the room. At the third supper they find you by the piano. "So you're the student," they say. "Shall we find out which of us they'll remember?"
text [1341dd] = 社交季总有一位宠儿，这一季在你来之前就有了：一位来自城里的年轻决斗冠军，英俊，迷人，早已习惯做全场最有意思的人。第三场晚宴上，对方在钢琴边找到了你。“原来你就是那个学生，”对方说，“我们来看看，他们会记住谁？”
# en: A crowded salon, a dashing young duelling champion holding court by the piano, a ring of admirers, a student watching from the doorway.
scene [966142] = 拥挤的沙龙里，一位潇洒的年轻决斗冠军在钢琴边被一圈仰慕者围着，一个学生在门口看着。
# en: Take them on
opt.left.label [285bb5] = 接受挑战
# en: You don't compete. You just tell the room a better story than they do, and the room turns, slowly, like a ship. The champion laughs, and bows, and says, "Well. Until the ball, then." The whole Season hears about it by morning.
opt.left.say.1 [b3c0cf] = 你没有去比。你只是给满屋子的人讲了一个比对方更好的故事，整个房间慢慢转了过来，像一艘船。冠军笑了，鞠了一躬，说：“好吧。舞会上见。”到早上，整个社交季都听说了。
# en: You try, and the champion is better at this than you, much better, and the room stays with them. Afterwards they find you on the terrace. "You were good," they say. "I've just had more practice." It's oddly comforting.
opt.left.say.2 [5fcdf5] = 你试了，可冠军比你擅长得多，满屋子的人一直围着对方。后来对方在露台上找到你。“你很好，”对方说，“我只是练得多些。”这话莫名地让人宽慰。
# en: Make a friend of them
opt.right.label [ad4a5d] = 和对方交个朋友
# en: "Why don't we not?" you say, and the champion looks surprised, then delighted. You spend the rest of the evening on the terrace, laughing at everyone else. The Season is a little disappointed. You've never had a better supper.
opt.right.say.1 [28e821] = “我们干脆别比了吧？”你说，冠军先是吃了一惊，接着高兴起来。那个晚上剩下的时间，你们俩在露台上取笑别人。社交季有点失望。你从没吃过比这更好的晚宴。

@event season_ball
# en: The Patrons' Ball
title [eea43b] = 赞助人舞会
# en: The Season ends with the patrons' ball. {flag(season_ball_star): Everyone knows by now that you're the one to watch, and the room turns when you come down the stairs.|You and the champion come down the stairs together, by arrangement, and the room can't decide which of you to look at.} The patrons are watching who you dance with, who you talk to, and how long you stay.
text [cd7c48] = 社交季以赞助人舞会收尾。{flag(season_ball_star): 如今人人都知道你是最值得看的那个，你走下楼梯时，整个大厅都转了过来。|你和冠军按事先说好的一起走下楼梯，整个大厅都拿不定主意该看谁。}赞助人们在看你和谁跳舞，和谁说话，待了多久。
# en: A grand townhouse ballroom, chandeliers blazing, couples whirling, a student pausing at the top of a staircase as the room turns to look.
scene [ce43eb] = 宅邸里富丽堂皇的舞厅，枝形吊灯通明，一对对舞伴旋转着，一个学生停在楼梯顶端，整个大厅转头看来。
# en: Stay till dawn, and dance with everyone
opt.left.label [78426a] = 待到天亮，和每个人跳舞
# en: You dance with the magistrate, the orchard woman, the champion and a very old duke who treads on your feet with great dignity. You leave at dawn with your shoes in your hand. By noon every patron in the valley knows your name.
opt.left.say.1 [9685df] = 你和地方法官跳了，和果园女士跳了，和冠军跳了，还和一位郑重其事地踩你脚的老公爵跳了。天亮时你拎着鞋离开。到中午，山谷里每一位赞助人都知道了你的名字。
# en: Leave at midnight, while it's still lovely
opt.right.label [b908e8] = 午夜离开，趁一切还美好
# en: You dance three dances, eat one perfect pastry and slip out at midnight into the cold. Walking up the hill under the stars, you think it was the loveliest night of your life, and you're glad you didn't stay long enough to spoil it.
opt.right.say.1 [d9c1ba] = 你跳了三支舞，吃了一块完美的酥饼，午夜时溜进了寒夜。星光下走回山上时，你觉得这是你一生中最美的夜晚，很庆幸没有待到把它弄糟。

@event season_friends
# en: Back Up the Hill
title [879a43] = 回到山上
# en: You come back to the common room after the Season, still in the good coat, and {f} and the others are in the middle of a joke you don't understand, about something that happened while you were away. {f} looks up. "Oh," {they} say{s}. "You're back. How were the patrons?" It's not unkind. It's not quite warm either.
text [75f645] = 社交季过后，你回到公共休息室，还穿着那件好外套，{f}和其他人正说到一个你听不懂的笑话，讲的是你不在时发生的事。{f}抬起头。“哦，”{they}说，“你回来了。赞助人们怎么样？”这话并不刻薄。只是也不太热络。
# en: A common room on an ordinary evening, a group of friends around a table mid-laugh, one student standing in the doorway still in a good coat.
scene [213ab2] = 一个平常夜晚的公共休息室，一群朋友围着桌子笑得正欢，一个学生站在门口，还穿着一件好外套。
# en: Tell them everything
opt.left.label [09f8b1] = 全都讲给他们听
# en: You tell them about the ceiling with the clouds and the duke who trod on your feet. They listen, politely. Somewhere in the middle, you notice {f} looking at the coat. Later, you hear the joke you missed. It was very good. You weren't there.
opt.left.say.1 [537548] = 你给他们讲画着云的天花板，讲踩你脚的公爵。他们礼貌地听着。讲到一半，你注意到{f}在看那件外套。后来，你听到了那个你错过的笑话。非常好笑。你当时不在。
# en: Take off the coat and sit down
opt.right.label [7e306e] = 脱下外套，坐下来
# en: You hang the coat on the back of the door and sit down and ask what the joke was. It takes three people to explain it and by the end you're all crying with laughter. {f} passes you the last of the biscuits, as if you'd never gone.
opt.right.say.1 [c9dac7] = 你把外套挂在门后，坐下来问那个笑话是什么。三个人才把它解释清楚，讲完时你们都笑出了眼泪。{f}把最后一块饼干递给你，好像你从没离开过。

@event season_price
# en: One Word From You
title [dd6928] = 你的一句话
# en: A letter from one of the patrons, very warm, very grateful for your company at the Season. And a small favour, between friends: the patron's grandson is applying to the Duelling Circle, and a word from you would settle it. You've seen the grandson duel. He's dreadful, and someone better will lose the place.
text [5d5b97] = 一位赞助人来信，非常热情，非常感谢你在社交季的陪伴。还有一个朋友之间的小人情：这位赞助人的孙子在申请加入决斗社，你一句话就能定下来。你见过那个孙子决斗。糟透了，会有更好的人因此失去名额。
# en: A letter with a crest open on a desk beside a quill and an inkpot, a student sitting back with arms crossed, frowning at it.
scene [a36395] = 书桌上摊着一封带纹章的信，旁边是羽毛笔和墨水瓶，一个学生抱着胳膊往后靠，皱眉看着它。
# en: Write the word
opt.left.label [a5525d] = 写下那句话
# en: You write two warm sentences and seal them. The grandson gets his place. A purse arrives with the patron's thanks. A second-year who should have had the place stops you in the corridor to ask if you know why they didn't. You say no.
opt.left.say.1 [b34686] = 你写了两句热情的话，封了口。那个孙子拿到了名额。一袋钱随着赞助人的谢意寄来了。一个本该拿到名额的二年级生在走廊里拦住你，问你知不知道自己为什么没被选上。你说不知道。
# en: Refuse, politely
opt.right.label [a4b1b7] = 礼貌地拒绝
# en: You write back that the Circle chooses its own, and you wouldn't presume. The patron's reply is short and cool. The second-year gets the place, and never knows you had anything to do with it. That seems right.
opt.right.say.1 [bb903c] = 你回信说决斗社自己挑人，你不敢僭越。赞助人的回信简短而冷淡。那个二年级生拿到了名额，永远不知道这件事和你有关。这样似乎才对。

@event season_end
# en: The Season Ends
title [da0371] = 社交季落幕
# en: Another card with a crest: the patrons hope you'll come again next Season, and the one after. {flag(season_sponsored): Lady Vaneshade has added a line in her own hand: "You did well. Do better."|flag(season_price_refused): One patron's name, you notice, is missing from the bottom.|It is signed by every patron in the valley.} You could have a place among them, if you wanted one, for good.
text [4f6700] = 又一张带纹章的请柬：赞助人们希望你下个社交季再来，还有再下一个。{flag(season_sponsored): 凡妮希德夫人亲笔添了一行：“你做得不错。还要更好。”|flag(season_price_refused): 你注意到，落款里少了一位赞助人的名字。|山谷里每一位赞助人都签了名。}只要你愿意，你可以在他们中间永远有一席之地。
# en: A student at a window with a heavy cream card in one hand, looking down at the lights of the town houses below the hill.
scene [bc4d99] = 一个学生站在窗边，一只手拿着厚重的奶油色请柬，俯瞰着山下宅邸的灯火。
# en: Take your place among them
opt.left.label [673de4] = 在他们中间占一席之地
# en: You write back that you'd be honoured. It's a door you can walk through now whenever you like, and you suspect you'll walk through it for the rest of your life. You're not sure, some evenings, whether that's a good thing.
opt.left.say.1 [9d55c1] = 你回信说你深感荣幸。如今这是一扇你随时可以走进去的门，你猜你这辈子都会走进去。有些夜晚，你不确定这是不是件好事。
# en: Let the Season go
opt.right.label [c6b7dc] = 让社交季过去吧
# en: You write back, kindly, that you've loved it and won't come again. Then you put the card in the drawer with the first one, and go down to the common room, where somebody has saved you a seat by the fire.
opt.right.say.1 [e48ccc] = 你和气地回信说，你很喜欢，但不会再来了。然后你把请柬和第一张放进同一个抽屉，下楼去公共休息室，那里有人在炉火边给你留了个座位。

@event fame_rivals_for_you
# en: Both Want You Tonight
title [6b8a12] = 今晚都想要你
# en: {a} and {b} can't stand each other, and both of them have asked you to the Newt tonight, separately, each making very sure the other heard. It isn't really about the Newt. It's about who gets to be seen walking in with you. They're both waiting for your answer, in front of half the courtyard.
text [e2e6b6] = {a}和{b}互相看不顺眼，而两人都约你今晚去蝾螈酒馆，分头约的，还都确保对方听见了。这其实和酒馆无关。而是关于谁能被人看见和你一起走进去。两人都当着半个庭院的人等你的回答。
# en: A courtyard at dusk, two students on either side of another, each holding an invitation and pointedly not looking at the other.
scene [c5c915] = 黄昏时的庭院，两个学生站在另一个学生的两边，各自拿着一份邀请，刻意不看对方。
# en: Go with {a}
opt.left.label [a0bfbe] = 和{a}去
# en: You go with {a}. {a} walks into the Newt a little taller than usual. {b} hears about it by breakfast, and greets you all week with a small, bright, terrible smile.
opt.left.say.1 [acadc4] = 你和{a}去了。{a}走进蝾螈酒馆时比平时挺拔了些。{b}早饭时就听说了，接下来一整个星期见到你都挂着一个小小的、明亮的、可怕的微笑。
# en: Turn both down, kindly
opt.right.label [609976] = 和气地都拒绝了
# en: You tell them both you're staying in, and do, and read in the common room till late. Each of them assumes you've gone with the other. By the time they work out you haven't, they've both forgiven you.
opt.right.say.1 [12fb49] = 你告诉他们俩你要待在宿舍，你也确实待在公共休息室里读书读到很晚。他们俩都以为你和另一个人去了。等他们发现你谁也没跟，两人都已经原谅你了。

@event fame_wedge
# en: Using You
title [5a97fc] = 在利用你
# en: {a} takes you aside. "I don't like how {b} is with you lately," {they} say{s}. "{b} never sat with us before you were famous. Now {b}'s everywhere you are, telling people you're close." {a} and {b} have been friends for years. You can see it costing {a} to say it.
text [d9c243] = {a}把你拉到一边。“我不喜欢{b}最近对你的样子，”{they}说，“你出名之前{b}从不和我们坐一起。现在你在哪儿{b}就在哪儿，到处跟人说你们很亲近。”{a}和{b}是多年的朋友。你看得出{a}说这话付出了代价。
# en: Two friends arguing in low voices in an alcove of a corridor, one of them gesturing back the way they came, where another student is waiting.
scene [53d19d] = 走廊的壁龛里，两个朋友压低声音争论，其中一个朝来的方向比画，另一个学生正在那边等着。
# en: Tell {a} {b} is a friend
opt.left.label [431708] = 告诉{a}，{b}是朋友
# en: "{b}'s always been kind to me," you say. {a} looks at you for a moment, then nods, stiffly. A week later you see {a} and {b} at opposite ends of the refectory. You're fairly sure that was you.
opt.left.say.1 [d23a6e] = “{b}一直对我很好，”你说。{a}看了你一会儿，然后生硬地点了点头。一个星期后，你看见{a}和{b}坐在膳堂的两头。你很确定那是因为你。
# en: Thank {a}, and watch
opt.right.label [c53ff6] = 谢谢{a}，然后留心看着
# en: You thank {a}, and say you'll keep an eye out, and you do. {b} is, you notice, a lot more interested in where you're going than in you. You start sitting with {a} again. {a} doesn't say "I told you so", which is generous.
opt.right.say.1 [e620e4] = 你谢过{a}，说你会留意，你也确实留意了。你注意到，{b}对你要去哪儿比对你本人感兴趣得多。你又开始和{a}坐在一起了。{a}没说“我早告诉过你”，真是大度。

@event fame_lend_your_name
# en: Your Name on It
title [27256b] = 署上你的名字
# en: {f} is running a stall at the next fair, selling enchanted bookmarks to pay for next year's books, and has had an idea: what if the sign said "as used by {player}"? You have, in fact, used one. Once. "It'd sell out in an hour," {f} says. "Go on. I can't afford the set texts otherwise."
text [48668d] = {f}要在下一次集市上摆个摊，卖施了魔法的书签，好凑齐明年的课本钱，还想出了个主意：要是招牌上写“{player}同款”呢？你确实用过一个。就一次。“一个小时就能卖光，”{f}说，“答应吧。不然我买不起指定教材。”
# en: A student holding a hand-painted sign for a stall at a fair, a pot of paint and a brush ready, looking hopefully at a friend.
scene [b27a2e] = 一个学生举着一块手绘的集市摊位招牌，颜料罐和刷子都备好了，满怀期待地看着一位朋友。
# en: Let {f} use your name
opt.left.label [753383] = 让{f}用你的名字
# en: The stall sells out in forty minutes, and {f} can buy every book on next year's list, with enough over for a new quill. {f} is giddy all evening. You're asked to sign three bookmarks, and you do, and one of them ends up in the library, where it marks a page for years.
opt.left.say.1 [8358ba] = 摊位四十分钟就卖光了，{f}买齐了明年书单上的每一本书，还剩下钱买了支新羽毛笔。{f}一整晚都乐得飘飘然。有人请你在三个书签上签名，你签了，其中一个最后进了图书馆，在一页书里夹了好多年。
# en: The sign draws a crowd, and then the bookmarks start marking the wrong pages, all of them, at once. People bring them back in handfuls, saying your name in a tone you don't like. {f} is mortified. You help pack up the stall.
opt.left.say.2 [4e312a] = 招牌引来了一大群人，接着书签开始夹错页，所有书签，同时。人们一把一把地把它们拿回来，说起你的名字时那语气你可不喜欢。{f}羞愧难当。你帮着收了摊。
# en: Help at the stall instead
opt.right.label [95412c] = 改去摊位帮忙
# en: You say your name stays off the sign, but you'll spend the afternoon behind the stall. You sell twenty bookmarks, badly, and {f} sells two hundred, very well. It's one of the nicest afternoons of the term.
opt.right.say.1 [a95c00] = 你说招牌上不写你的名字，但你可以在摊位后面待一下午。你卖出了二十个书签，卖得很笨拙，{f}卖出了两百个，卖得非常好。那是这学期最愉快的下午之一。

@event fame_best_friend_claims
# en: Your Best Friend, Twice
title [db400c] = 你的挚友，两个
# en: The gossip column this week has two letters. In one, {a} writes that {a} is "{player}'s closest friend, as everyone knows". In the other, {b} writes exactly the same thing about {b}. They can't stand each other. Everyone has read both, and everyone is waiting to see what you'll say.
text [44565b] = 这周的八卦专栏有两封来信。一封里，{a}写道{a}是“{player}最亲密的朋友，人尽皆知”。另一封里，{b}写{b}的话一模一样。他们俩互相看不顺眼。大家都读了两封，都在等着看你会怎么说。
# en: A gossip column on a corridor board with two paragraphs circled in different inks, two students on opposite sides of the corridor, arms folded.
scene [5a4aa0] = 走廊布告板上的八卦专栏里，两段话被用不同颜色的墨水圈了出来，两个学生站在走廊两边，抱着胳膊。
# en: Write to the column yourself
opt.left.label [750561] = 亲自给专栏写信
# en: You write one line: that your closest friends are the two people who wrote in, and that they should compare notes, since they have so much in common. The column prints it. {a} and {b} are furious with you, together, which is a start.
opt.left.say.1 [e2ab37] = 你只写了一句：你最亲密的朋友就是写信来的那两位，他们俩该好好交流一下心得，毕竟有那么多共同点。专栏登了出来。{a}和{b}都气坏了，冲着你，一起，这也算是个开始。
# en: Your letter is meant to be funny and reads as if you were choosing. Each of them decides you meant the other. The column runs a follow-up, with diagrams.
opt.left.say.2 [572707] = 你的信本想写得风趣，读起来却像是在二选一。两人都认定你说的是对方。专栏又出了一篇续文，还配了示意图。
# en: Say nothing at all
opt.right.label [0e1895] = 一言不发
# en: You say nothing, to anyone. The column decides your closest friend must be someone else entirely, and spends a fortnight guessing who. {a} and {b}, united at last, both think it's very unfair.
opt.right.say.1 [c048f6] = 你对谁都一言不发。专栏断定你最亲密的朋友一定另有其人，猜了两个星期是谁。{a}和{b}终于站到了一起，都觉得这非常不公平。

@event fame_after_you
# en: What They Found Instead
title [147c59] = 他们找到了别的
# en: When you were famous, {a} and {b} used to argue over who got to walk with you. Now nobody fights over you, and you see the two of them on the lakeshore path in the evening, walking together, very close, laughing at something. They haven't noticed you on the bench. They don't need to.
text [8f8d30] = 你出名的时候，{a}和{b}常常争着要和你一起散步。如今没人为你争了，你在傍晚的湖岸小路上看见他们俩一起散步，挨得很近，为什么事笑着。他们没注意到长凳上的你。他们也不需要注意。
# en: A quiet lakeshore path in the evening, two students walking close together ahead, laughing, a third student watching them from a bench.
scene [9a57d1] = 傍晚安静的湖岸小路上，两个学生挨得很近地走在前面，笑着，第三个学生坐在长凳上看着他们。
# en: Call out to them
opt.left.label [b99090] = 叫住他们
# en: You call out, and they turn, and wave, and come over, and the three of you sit on the bench until it's dark. They're kind. You can see, though, that they'd have been just as happy walking on.
opt.left.say.1 [704a75] = 你喊了一声，他们转过身，挥挥手，走过来，三个人在长凳上一直坐到天黑。他们很友善。不过你看得出，他们就这么接着走下去也一样开心。
# en: Let them walk on
opt.right.label [1172b6] = 让他们走吧
# en: You let them walk on, and watch them go round the curve of the shore until you can't see them. It's a strange feeling, being the reason two people met, and not being needed any more. Mostly it's a good one.
opt.right.say.1 [d70e8a] = 你让他们走了，看着他们绕过湖岸的弯道，直到看不见。成了两个人相遇的原因，却不再被需要，这感觉很奇怪。大部分时候，是种好的感觉。
